{
 "esm": "1.1.0",
 "metadata": {
  "name": "NoahSnowCompaction",
  "description": "Snow-pack compaction in WRF's unified Noah land-surface model, SUBROUTINE SNOWPACK of phys/module_sf_noahlsm.F (WRF 4.8, sf_surface_physics = 2): the densification of an existing pack under its own weight, and the melt water it retains once its surface reaches freezing. THE PHYSICS. Anderson's compaction equation (NOAA Technical Report NWS 19, eq. 3.29) makes the density at a point in the pack grow exponentially in the water equivalent lying above it, at a rate that rises with temperature and falls with the density already reached: d(rho)/dt = rho c1 exp(ctemp tavgc - c2 rho) w. Koren's 1995 approximate solution integrates that over the DEPTH of the pack rather than over time, so the pack-mean density after a step of dtsec is sndens (exp(x) - 1)/x with x = bfac esdcx, and bfac carries the whole of the step length. WRF evaluates (exp(x) - 1)/x through its Taylor series to four terms, because the closed form is 0/0 at x = 0 -- its own comment says so at length. Once the snow surface reaches freezing a second process runs: 13 % of the pack's pore space per day fills with melt water, which relaxes the density towards that of water. The depth is not integrated at all; it is the quotient esd/sndens at the end. HOW IT JOINS AN ODE DOCUMENT. SNOWPACK is an in-place sub-step, not a rate -- Koren's solution is a closed form in the step length and there is no dt-free rate inside it -- so this component takes dtsec as a PARAMETER OF THE SCHEME and exposes `dsndens_dt` = increment/dtsec as the observed a consumer integrating the pack adds, which is the same shape WSM6's operator-split stages use. A mounting document must prescribe dtsec and couple it in; it is not the integrator's step, and the result differs from the dtsec -> 0 limit by xarg/3 + O(xarg^2), measured at 2.2e-6 relative at WRF's own 60 s step. WHY THE INCREMENT IS NEVER FORMED AS A DIFFERENCE. The compaction moves the density by a factor 1 + O(dtsec): 9.4e-5 of it at WRF's 60 s step on the wettest reference column, 4.5e-9 on the thinnest pack, and 7.5e-17 at a 1e-6 s step. Writing the increment as sndens_out - sndens_in therefore returns round-off at any step small enough to be a dt -> 0 reference, and writing it as sndens (pexp - 1) is no better because pexp is stored as 1 + O(1e-13). Both the component and its reference form it instead as sndens_in pexp_m1 + dw (1 - dsx), an exact rearrangement in which nothing cancels, and the reference is taken from a publisher that emits the series sum BEFORE WRF adds one to it (FORTRAN_BUGS N99, the same move N97 and N98 forced for the frozen-soil sink and the melt rate one routine over). REFERENCE STEP. Because nothing is differenced there is no cancellation floor pushing the reference step down, and the usual 'shrink dt until the increment is the derivative' recipe is actively WRONG here: the compaction increment is proportional to dtsec, so a SMALLER step puts LESS of an assertion's budget on the physics. The primary regime is therefore WRF's own 60 s step, where a rel 1e-9 assertion on sndens_out tests the compaction to between 1e-5 and 2e-1 of itself, and a second regime at 1e-6 s pins the tendency in the dt -> 0 limit, where the naive form would be 100 % noise. NOT TRANSCRIBED: the whole UA_PHYS branch (the snomlt-limited retention fraction), which is off in every reference run; SNOW_NEW, which sets the density of freshly fallen snow and is a separate routine; and the caller's own esd > 0 guard, which belongs to SNOPAC and is reported here as the indicator `pack_present`. MEASURED AGREEMENT: every one of the 1659 assertions passes at rel 3e-15, a few ulps of binary64, over all 134 tests of both regimes; the published bound is the 1e-9 the project sets for a double-precision kernel reference, and the six orders between them are headroom, not accuracy that was needed.",
  "license": "MIT",
  "tags": [
   "wrf",
   "land-surface",
   "noah",
   "snow",
   "snow-density",
   "compaction"
  ],
  "references": [
   {
    "citation": "Anderson, E. A. (1976). A point energy and mass balance model of a snow cover. NOAA Technical Report NWS 19, equation (3.29)."
   },
   {
    "citation": "Koren, V., Schaake, J., Mitchell, K., Duan, Q.-Y., Chen, F., & Baker, J. M. (1999). A parameterization of snowpack and frozen ground intended for NCEP weather and climate models. J. Geophys. Res., 104(D16), 19569-19585."
   },
   {
    "citation": "Kojima, K. (1967). Densification of seasonal snow cover. Physics of Snow and Ice, 1(2), 929-952."
   },
   {
    "citation": "wrf-model/WRF v4.8.0 phys/module_sf_noahlsm.F SUBROUTINE SNOWPACK lines 3417-3549, called from SNOPAC at line 3403 (cold-season reference run instrumented in ctessum-claude/WRF branch earthsciml-instrumented-noahcold)."
   }
  ]
 },
 "models": {
  "NoahSnowCompaction": {
   "subsystems": {
    "par": {
     "ref": "./noah_parameters.esm"
    }
   },
   "variables": {
    "esd": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "m",
     "default": 0.06,
     "description": "Water equivalent of the pack that SNOWPACK is handed, WRF's ESD -- the SWE AFTER SNOPAC has taken this step's sublimation and melt out of it, not the pack the step started with. Consumer-supplied, because it is the snow-energy component's output that sets it."
    },
    "sndens_in": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "1",
     "default": 0.2,
     "description": "Snow density entering the compaction, as a fraction of the density of water (WRF's SNDENS; its own comment reads 'G/CM3=DIMENSIONLESS FRACTION OF H2O DENSITY', and the two are numerically equal). This is NOT the density implied by the dumped snow state: SFLX resets it to SNEQV/SNOWH and then, on a snowing step, SNOW_NEW moves it again before SNOPAC is reached, so it is supplied by the consumer and is published from inside SNOWPACK for the tests."
    },
    "tsnow": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "K",
     "default": 265.0,
     "description": "Snow surface temperature, WRF's TSNOW dummy, which SNOPAC fills with the skin temperature T1 its own energy balance has just produced. Its position relative to the freezing point selects the melt-water retention branch."
    },
    "tsoil": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "K",
     "default": 268.0,
     "description": "Soil temperature at the top of the soil column, WRF's TSOIL dummy, which SNOPAC fills with YY = stc[1] - 0.5 ssoil zsoil[1]/df1 -- the linearised surface value the soil-heat closure forms, not stc[1] itself."
    },
    "dtsec": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "s",
     "default": 60.0,
     "description": "The sub-step the compaction is applied over, WRF's DTSEC. A PARAMETER OF THE SCHEME, not the integrator's step: Koren's solution is a closed form in the step length, so the increment it returns is O(dtsec) and there is no dt-free rate hiding inside it. Prescribe it in the mounting document and couple it in, exactly as WSM6's dtcld is prescribed; a parent test cannot set a mounted component's parameter."
    },
    "tsnowc": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "K",
     "description": "Snow surface temperature above freezing, WRF's TSNOWC = TSNOW - 273.15."
    },
    "tsoilc": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "K",
     "description": "Soil surface temperature above freezing, WRF's TSOILC."
    },
    "tavgc": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "K",
     "description": "Mean temperature of the pack above freezing, the arithmetic mean of the snow surface and the soil below it. It is the only temperature the compaction sees: warm snow compacts fast."
    },
    "esdc": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "cm",
     "description": "Water equivalent in SNOWPACK's own simulation units, centimetres."
    },
    "esdcx": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "cm",
     "description": "Water equivalent floored at par.snow_esdc_min, WRF's ESDCX. It bounds the overburden a vanishing pack is allowed to feel. It is used ONLY in the compaction argument; the depth diagnostic at the end uses the unfloored esdc, and that distinction matters for the nine reference steps whose pack is below the floor."
    },
    "dthr": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "h",
     "description": "The sub-step in hours, the unit SNOWPACK's rate constants are expressed in."
    },
    "comp_exponent": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "description": "Anderson's compaction exponent, snow_ctemp tavgc - snow_c2 sndens_in: compaction accelerates with temperature and is suppressed by the density already reached. Declared dimensionless in its own right so that the exponential is handed a quantity of scale one."
    },
    "bfac": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1/cm",
     "description": "The compaction rate per centimetre of overlying water equivalent, over the whole sub-step: dthr snow_c1 exp(comp_exponent). It is proportional to dtsec, which is where all of the step dependence enters."
    },
    "xarg": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "description": "bfac esdcx, the dimensionless compaction of the pack's BASE over the sub-step. It is proportional to dtsec: 6.7e-6 at WRF's 60 s on a 6 cm pack at -12 C, and 1.1e-13 at 1e-6 s."
    },
    "pexp_m1": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "description": "The mean compaction over the DEPTH of the pack, minus one. Anderson's equation makes the density at depth w grow as exp(bfac w), so the pack-mean factor is the depth average of that over 0 <= w <= esdcx, i.e. (exp(xarg) - 1)/xarg; WRF replaces it by its Taylor series to IPOL = 4 terms because the closed form is 0/0 at xarg = 0, and this is that series MINUS ONE, xarg/2 + xarg^2/6 + xarg^3/24 + xarg^4/120, written in WRF's own Horner nesting. It is the whole of the compaction signal and it is the quantity the tests are pinned to: (exp(x)-1)/x itself differs from 1 by O(x), so at any small step the stored pexp is 1 + O(1e-13) and carries no information at all."
    },
    "pexp": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "description": "WRF's PEXP, 1 + pexp_m1."
    },
    "dsx_raw": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "description": "Compacted density before the density limits, sndens_in pexp."
    },
    "dsx_clamp": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "description": "Which density limit fired on dsx_raw: 0 neither, 1 capped at snow_dens_max, 2 floored at snow_dens_min. WRF applies the two tests in sequence, cap first, and because the cap is above the floor the second test can only fire on a value that the first left alone, so the two are mutually exclusive. UNTESTED, not merely unused: neither limit fires anywhere in the cold-season reference set, all 67 of whose compaction calls report 0."
    },
    "dsx": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "description": "Compacted density after the limits, WRF's DSX."
    },
    "ddsx": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "description": "The compaction increment, dsx - sndens_in, written CANCELLATION-FREE as sndens_in pexp_m1 where no limit fired. That is an exact algebraic rearrangement, not an approximation, and it is the reason this component can be referenced at any step size: the increment is 7.5e-17 of the density at a 1e-6 s step, so forming it as a difference would return pure round-off. Where a limit fires the increment is a difference against a constant and is formed directly."
    },
    "wet": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "description": "Indicator, 1 where the snow surface has reached freezing and SNOWPACK runs its melt-water retention branch. 29 of the 67 compaction calls in the cold-season reference set; the other 38 are dry."
    },
    "dw": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "description": "Fraction of the pack's pore space filled by retained melt water over the sub-step, snow_dwrate dthr/24: 13 % per day. Zero on the dry branch. Like bfac it is proportional to dtsec. The UA_PHYS alternative, DW = min(DW, 0.13 snomltc/(esdcx + 0.13 snomltc)), is NOT transcribed -- UA_PHYS is off in every reference run."
    },
    "sndens_wet": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "description": "Density after retention and before its own cap, WRF's dsx (1 - dw) + dw: a relaxation of the density towards 1 at rate snow_dwrate, i.e. towards pure water, which is what saturating the pore space with melt water would give."
    },
    "wet_capped": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "description": "Indicator for WRF's second density cap, applied only on the retention branch. UNTESTED: no reference step reaches it, because 0.40 is far above any density these four columns produce."
    },
    "dsndens": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "description": "The total density increment over the sub-step, ddsx + dw (1 - dsx), formed without ever differencing two densities. On the dry branch this is the compaction alone."
    },
    "sndens_out": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "description": "Density SNOWPACK returns, sndens_in + dsndens."
    },
    "snowh_out": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m",
     "description": "Depth SNOWPACK returns, esd/sndens_out. It is a PURE DIAGNOSTIC of the water equivalent and the density, with no step dependence of its own, and it uses the UNFLOORED esd. SNOWPACK's SNOWH argument is declared INTENT(INOUT) but its incoming value is never read: SNOWHC = SNOWH*100 at the top of the routine is overwritten by SNOWHC = ESDC/SNDENS at the bottom before anything uses it (FORTRAN_BUGS N100), so the depth entering the routine is not an input of this component."
    },
    "dsndens_dt": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1/s",
     "description": "dsndens/dtsec -- the density tendency a consumer integrating the pack must add. This is the operator-split form: SNOWPACK is an in-place sub-step, not a rate, so the way it joins an ODE document is as increment over sub-step, exactly as WSM6's stages do. Its dtsec -> 0 limit is analytic and clean, sndens_in snow_c1 exp(comp_exponent_0) esdcx/(2*3600) + wet snow_dwrate (1 - sndens_in)/86400 with comp_exponent_0 the exponent at dtsec-free bfac; the finite-step value exceeds it by xarg/3 + O(xarg^2), which is 2.2e-6 relative at WRF's 60 s and 3.7e-14 at 1e-6 s."
    },
    "pack_present": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "description": "Indicator for the caller's guard: SNOPAC calls SNOWPACK only where esd > 0, and where it does not it sets the depth, the density and the cover to zero and the snow conductivity to one instead. Reported here so a consumer knows when this component applies; it is asserted against the reference's own record of whether SNOWPACK ran."
    }
   },
